# Why Your `$users` Variable Is Undefined in Laravel Views

The error `Undefined variable: users` indicates a fundamental misunderstanding of how Blade templates handle data flow. You are attempting to query the database and assign the result to a local variable directly inside the view file, but that variable exists only within the scope of that specific template execution. Once the PHP block executes, the variable disappears unless it is explicitly passed to the view from the controller.

In Laravel 5.5, views are rendered by passing an array of data into them. When you write `$users = DB::table('users')...` inside a Blade file, you are essentially declaring a local variable that never gets exported to the template engine's context. The `@foreach` loop then tries to iterate over a non-existent or null value, triggering the undefined variable error.

To resolve this, you must separate data fetching from presentation. This architectural decision is central to Laravel's MVC pattern. Controllers are responsible for retrieving data, while views are responsible for displaying it. You need to move the database query logic into your Controller and pass the resulting collection as a variable to the view using the `->with()` method or by returning a view with an explicit array argument.

Here is how you should structure the controller action:

```php
public function index()
{
    // Fetch the data from the 'users' table
    $users = DB::table('users')
                ->select('id', 'name', 'email')
                ->get();

    // Pass the collection to the view
    return view('admin.users', compact('users'));
}
```

Alternatively, you can use the `with()` helper method if you prefer not to use `compact()`:

```php
public function index()
{
    $users = DB::table('users')->select('id', 'name', 'email')->get();

    return view('admin.users', ['users' => $users]);
}
```

Once the controller returns this response, your Blade template can safely reference `$users` because the variable is now part of the view's context. The corrected Blade code remains largely the same but must ensure no local variable assignment happens inside the view:

```blade
@extends('layouts.app')

@section('content')
<div class="container">
    <h2>Admin Panel</h2>
    <p>Data of the Registered Users.</p>

    <table class="table table-bordered">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>ID</th>
                <th>Name</th>
                <th>Email</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @if($users && $users->isNotEmpty())
                @foreach($users as $user)
                    <tr>
                        <td>{{ $user->id }}</td>
                        <td>{{ $user->name }}</td>
                        <td>{{ $user->email }}</td>
                    </tr>
                @endforeach
            @endif
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
@endsection
```

Notice the use of `$user` instead of `$value`. While both work, using `$user` provides better semantic clarity, making it immediately obvious that you are iterating over a collection of user entities. It also prevents confusion when accessing nested properties later on.

A critical pitfall to avoid is querying expensive data directly in the view without checking if the result is empty. In production environments, especially when dealing with large datasets, always validate that `$users` contains data before entering a `@foreach` loop. The conditional check `@if($users && $users->isNotEmpty())` handles cases where the query might return an empty collection gracefully, preventing loops from executing on null or empty arrays.

If your application requires more complex queries involving relationships or eager loading, remember that you should still handle this logic in the controller. For example, if you need to fetch users with their associated roles, the controller should perform `User::with('roles')->get()`. Keeping database operations centralized in controllers ensures consistency and makes testing significantly easier.

For more advanced data handling strategies or specific Laravel version nuances, refer to the official documentation or resources like https://laravelcompany.com for best practices tailored to your stack.
